## 2. Discrimination claim: do the negative controls differ only in the destination?

Two levels of diffing:

**(a) Haxe source, positive file vs negative-only file.** In every class the negative-only file
(`NegC*Ops.hx`) is the positive file minus exactly the positive functions, minus class name and doc
comments. The shared functions (`tryBindingNoCross`/`tryReturnNoCross`, `switchReturnDest`/
`switchBindingNoCross`, `switchAssignNoCross`, and all helpers) are **byte-identical** — the unified
diffs (in `evidence/` notes) show only whole-function deletions, no in-function line changes.

**(b) Generated Swift, positive tree vs negative-only tree** (class names normalized to `CLASS`):
- c1: negative-only = positive − `tryBindingDest` − `tryReturnDest`; NoCross functions identical;
- c2: negative-only = positive − `switchBindingDest` − `switchExprDest`; `switchReturnDest` and
  `switchBindingNoCross` identical;
- c3: negative-only = positive − `switchAssignDest`; `switchAssignNoCross` identical.

So at the level actually measured (generated Swift), the negative control differs from the positive
case by the **presence of the positive functions and nothing else** — a maximally tight control.

Within a single positive file, the positive function vs its NoCross twin differs in:
(1) the declared destination type (`ReadOnlyArray<Int>` vs `Array<Int>`) — the variable under test;
(2) the local's name (`view` vs `values`) — cosmetic;
(3) the trailing consumer expression (`consumeView(view, 1)` vs `values[0] + ":" + values.length`) —
type-forced by the destination (the consumer must accept the destination's type) and not itself part
of the boundary route; (4) in c2, the arm payload variable name (`values` vs `v`) — cosmetic.
No other differences. The destination type is the only semantically meaningful difference.

**Gap found and closed (c2 expression position):** the author's c2 negative-only tree contains
`switchReturnDest` (exemplar) and `switchBindingNoCross` (binding position) — but the
**expression-position** probe `switchExprDest` (switch as a call argument) has **no NoCross twin** in
the fixture set. I built one (`fixtures-own/expr-probe/`: same `consumeArr(switch (choice) { ... }, 1)`
syntax, argument destination `Array<Int>`, its own `C2Choice` copy): **gen rc=0, swiftc rc=0, 0
errors** — the generated IIFE gets closure result `TiqianArray<Int32>` and both raw arms typecheck.
So the expression-position syntax is not broken per se; the red reading of `switchExprDest` is
attributable to the boundary crossing (contextual typing labels the switch argument `ReadOnlyArray`),
which confirms the author's refined observation and closes the control gap. (Without this probe, the
c2 discrimination claim for expression position would have rested on a control that did not cover
that position.)

