## 7. Verdict

**CONFIRMED.** Every reported row (gen rc, swiftc rc, distinct error sites) reproduced exactly from
independent copies, with byte-identical generated trees and byte-identical swiftc stderr. The three
side claims — green `switchReturnDest` exemplar with correct `ReadOnlyArray(...)` conversions;
red expression-position probe (contextual inference labels the switch argument with the parameter
type); swiftc per-closure error suppression making the c2 count of 2 an under-count of 4 genuinely
defective arm lines — were each verified independently (the suppression via three probes, including
fixing the real c2 first arms and observing the previously suppressed second arms error).
The discrimination claim holds: negative controls differ from the positives by exactly the positive
functions at the measured (generated) level, and by destination type (plus type-forced consumer code
and cosmetic names) at the source level. Two documented reservations: (1) c2's expression-position
probe lacked a NoCross twin in the author's set — supplied by this x-check, green; (2) c2 and c3
discriminate routes, not fix-classes, whenever a change lands in the shared `armLines`
(author's own note, consistent with all observations).

